keep the draw ahead of the housekeeping

Slowdowns were still reported in play on every core, as hitches rather
than a steady loss of speed. Nothing here has been traced on the handheld
yet; these are the four places, read out of the code, where the thread that
draws and presents was made to wait on, or to share a CPU with, work that
could land a frame later without anyone seeing it.

- the rewind worker is spawned from the emulator thread, and Linux hands a
  thread its creator's nice value. The emulator sets itself to -8, so the
  worker's copy, XOR and LZ4 of every snapshot -- 529 KB on a SNES, every
  eleven frames -- ran at -8 too, above the draw at 0. That is the shape of
  nes-snes.md's "rewind on 20-30 missed a latch, off 7-27". It, the state
  writer, the cart-face workers, the prefetch, the link art, the autosave
  writer and the font warm-up now run at nice 10, and the draw at -4: above
  the system's own daemons, below the emulator, audio and input.
- the rewind worker copied every state it is handed a second time before
  diffing it: 529 KB a SNES snapshot for nothing. It takes ownership now.
  The hand-off stays a blocking send -- upstream's tests pin that every
  push is kept, in order -- which also makes a starved worker hold the
  emulator back rather than fall ever further behind.
- the autosave asked the emulator for its state and its save RAM from the
  draw, once a minute: up to a frame waiting for the core to finish the one
  it was on, and then a serialize. A snapshot that can travel to another
  thread (`Snapshot::detach`, which the emulator's does) is now asked from
  the autosave's own writer thread. Stubs that cannot travel keep the old
  path, so the existing tests are unchanged.
- the Flip's battery is `/customer/app/axp_test`, a process, and it was run
  and waited for on the draw every ten seconds. Only the first reading is
  waited for now; each poll after answers with the last reading and starts
  the next one behind it on a background thread.

Tests, run as armv7 under qemu: niceness's two pin that a background thread
lowers only itself and that a spawned thread inherits unless it says
otherwise -- qemu-user passes setpriority through to the host kernel, so
that is Linux's behaviour rather than a mock's. flush.rs gains a test that
the autosave asks a travelling snapshot off the calling thread and still
writes the resume; rewind gains a round trip through push and push_owned;
device.rs gains one that a slow battery tool is waited for only once.

Applies on top of 0040, upstream 91499a9.

